  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nAn elongated lobe of vorticity continues to push through the \nNorthern Plains aloft, keeping the chance (~10%) for sprinkles going \nfor the rest of the afternoon. No break in the chance for sprinkles \nthrough the evening and into the overnight hours as the low levels \nremain saturated, especially as more widespread low level stratus \npushes into the area. Perhaps a few flurries are possible with the \nsprinkles as temperatures cool to near to just below freezing at the \nsurface tonight.\n\nThursday will be the first noticeably cooler day with the post \nfrontal airmass in place. Should see highs warm to the low 40s to \nlow to mid 30s, if the low level stratus breaks up enough to allow \nfor sufficient diurnal heating. If cloud cover prevails, highs could \nremain in the 30s. Surface ridging will be sliding into the area as \nwell, keeping winds light, though just barely strong enough to \nresult in wind chills down in the 30s to potentially upper 20s in a \nfew locations during the daylight hours. Some sprinkles also remain \npossible beneath the stratus as well. With the ridge parked over the \narea, low temperatures look to fall to the coldest temperatures we \nhave seen so far this season, with lows falling to the low to mid \n20s. Some locations could see lows fall into the upper teens. With \nvery light to nearly calm winds in place, little if any wind chill \nwill be felt. Could see lows fall further but there remains \nuncertainty regarding cloud cover.\n\nFriday will be another cooler day with near to below seasonable \nhighs in the 30s and 40s. Breezy winds look to return to locations \nalong and west of the James River as the previously mentioned \nsurface ridge begins to slide eastwards, tightening low level \npressure gradients. Low temperatures will remain in the 20s Friday \nnight, but should be a little bit warmer than Thursday night's lows \nas winds turn to out of the southeast and remain elevated through \nthe night.\n\nUpper level ridging remains on track to push eastwards into the \nPlains for the weekend. This will begin a small warming trend with \nhighs warming back into the 40s across the area. Temperatures will \ncontinue to warm through early next week with highs in the 40s and \n50s for Monday and Tuesday. No rain is expected for any of these \ndays.\n\nMedium range guidance continues to show some variance regarding the \nupper level pattern for the middle of next week. There is some \nconsensus that broad troughing from the western CONUS will push \neastwards for this period of time. The ensembles not only support \nthis troughing, but they show increased probabilities for light \nrain, up to a 20-50% chance for rainfall totals to exceed a \nhundredth of an inch. Their probabilities lower for rainfall amounts \nexceeding a tenth of an inch. Could still see these chances for rain \npersist into the end of next week, but details remain uncertain.",
